## Frequently Asked Questions

### Can therapy help with everyday anxiety?

Yes. Therapy can help you identify anxiety triggers, examine unhelpful thought patterns, practice coping skills, and change behaviors that may be maintaining anxiety. The approach depends on your concerns, preferences, and treatment goals. CBT is one well-studied approach commonly used for anxiety disorders.

### What type of therapy is best for anxiety?

There is no single approach that is best for everyone. Cognitive behavioral therapy is commonly used for anxiety disorders and has substantial research support. Other approaches may also be appropriate depending on the type of anxiety, your circumstances, and your preferences. A qualified mental health professional can help you explore suitable options.

### How do I know if my anxiety is more than everyday stress?

Consider how often anxiety occurs, how difficult it is to manage, how long it lasts, and whether it interferes with daily activities. If worry persists, feels overwhelming, or affects your sleep, work, school, or relationships, consider discussing it with a qualified professional. Only an appropriate assessment can determine whether you have an anxiety disorder.

### What should I expect during anxiety counseling?

Your therapist will generally ask about your concerns, symptoms, daily experiences, and goals. Depending on your needs, sessions may involve exploring triggers, identifying thought patterns, practicing coping strategies, and reviewing progress. You can ask questions and discuss your comfort level throughout the process.

### Can I manage anxiety without therapy?

Some people find that lifestyle adjustments and coping strategies help them manage occasional anxiety. Consistent sleep, physical activity, stress-management techniques, and reducing caffeine when it worsens symptoms may be useful. However, if anxiety is persistent, severe, or interfering with your life, professional support may be appropriate.

### How long does therapy for anxiety take?

The duration varies. It depends on the nature of your concerns, their impact on daily life, your goals, the therapy approach, and how you respond to treatment. Your therapist can discuss a reasonable plan with you and review progress as you go. No specific timeline or outcome can be guaranteed.

### Does Riaz Counseling offer anxiety therapy in Houston?

